Drop react-native-ios-utilities' reference to RCTRootContentView.

React Native 0.85 wraps RCTRootContentView's entire implementation in
`#ifndef RCT_REMOVE_LEGACY_ARCH`. With the New Architecture enabled that define
is set, so React/Base/RCTRootContentView.m compiles to an object file defining
zero symbols and the class effectively does not exist. Linking the app then
fails:

    Undefined symbols for architecture arm64:
      "_OBJC_CLASS_$_RCTRootContentView", referenced from:
           in libreact-native-ios-utilities.a(RCTView+Helpers.o)

This is not a build-configuration problem: it reproduces with both the prebuilt
React-Core framework and a from-source build.

The reference is dead code under the New Architecture. `closestParentReactContentView`
exists solely as the last-resort fallback inside `closestParentReactTouchHandler`,
whose only consumer is react-native-ios-context-menu calling `.cancel()` on an
old-architecture RCTTouchHandler. Fabric does not install RCTTouchHandler on the
view hierarchy, so that lookup already returns nil at runtime and the call is
already a no-op. Removing it changes no behaviour; it only stops Swift emitting
a reference to a class that is no longer compiled.

The sibling `closestParentRootView` also names a legacy-guarded class
(RCTRootView) but has no consumers, so the linker dead-strips it and it needs no
change.

Upstream cannot help here: react-native-ios-utilities 5.2.0 and
react-native-ios-context-menu 3.2.1 are both the latest releases and both were
published 2025-09-28.

--- node_modules/react-native-ios-utilities/ios/Sources/Extensions+Helpers/RCTView+Helpers.swift
